  "notes": [
    "The corpus is synthetic. These numbers show the system behaves as designed on cases whose ground truth is known by construction; they do not show it works on real-world noise, because the corpus contains none.",
    "Run against the deterministic mock provider, so cost is genuinely $0.00 and latency measures Bishop's own code rather than a model round trip. Verdicts come from arithmetic over detector scores \u2014 see src/bishop/models/mock.py.",
    "23 alerts is a smoke test, not a benchmark. One alert moving changes accuracy by 4 points.",
    "100% accuracy on 20 alerts is not a generalisation claim, and should not be read as one. The corpus was written first and the fusion thresholds were then tuned against it, so this measures internal consistency \u2014 the detectors, the mitigating-context rules and the label definitions agreeing with each other \u2014 rather than performance on unseen data. A held-out set is the obvious next thing this project needs.",
    "Technique recall is 81%: some techniques a labelled alert should surface are not produced by any detector. That gap is real and docs/COVERAGE.md shows exactly where it is."
  ]
